  "strategy": "1. **Approach and rationale**\n   - Central idea: Refbose's function with structure- aware optimization. Erd\u0151s problem seeks to minimize the maximum overlap of a function with its shifts. The optimal solution likely has a piecewise constant structure with multiple peaks separated by valleys. We will use a multi- resolution approach to detect and refine these features.\n   - Key limitation: Continuous optimization on discrete grids must preserve integral constraints while maintaining function values in [0,1]. We use likelihood-based optimization to identify where to \"pluck\" features from neighboring grids.\n   - Distinction: Unlike previous methods, we introduce a feature extraction mechanism that transfers periodic structures between resolutions. Computational bottleneck: The correlation matrix grows quadratically with resolution, requiring a block-diagonal caching strategy.\n\n2. **Mathematical specification**\n   - Variables: Function representation h(x) defined via grid samples h \u2208 \u211d\u207f\u207a\u00b2\u1d5b\u1d49\u207f\u00b2\u1d5b\u1d49\u207f with \u2261sum(h) = n/2 * dx \u2261\n   - Objective: Minimize max\u2096 \u27e8h, 1 - h(x+k)\u27e9\n   - Formulation: We use mode=\"full\" autocorrelation as the metric: C(h) = ||corr(h, 1-h)||/dx\n   - Constrained optimization: Use FCN with dual variables for sum(h) = 1.28e4.5e5...\n   - Feature extraction: Identify peaks via likelihood: L(i) = [1 - A(i)] + \u03c4\u00b7I(i) where \u03c4 is temporal gap penalty\n\n3. **Implementation procedure**\n   a. **Multi-resolution initialization:**\n      - Start with initial_h_values grid at n=425\n      - Generate coarse grids via downsampling and peak convolution\n   b. **Periodicity detection:**\n      - Compute discrete Fourier transform: F(\u03c9) = \u2211h[i]e\u1d62\u03c9\n      - Extract periodic components: P(\u03c9) = |F(\u03c9)|/\u221an\n      - Identify dominant frequencies for feature transfer\n   c. **Likelihood-based optimization:**\n      - Define loss: L(h) = \u2211\u2096 max(t, C(h)) \n      - Augment with periodic residual terms: R(h) = \u2211\u2225h(x) - h(x-p)\u2225\n      - Optimization path: \n        1. Feature extraction phase: identify candidate peaks\n        2. Transfer learning phase: embed features from coarser grids\n        3. Refinement phase: minimize smooth_max objective\n   d. **Validation:**\n        - Constraint repair: post-processing projection to [0,1]\n        - Feasibility check: verify sum(h)\u2248n/2\n        - Objective: re-evaluate with np.correlate\n\n4. **Search choices:**\n   - Grid resolutions: [425, 800, 1600]\n   - Candidate generation: Randomized peak selection for each resolution\n   - Optimization bounds: Active set size 2000\n   - Feature penalty: \u03c4 \u2208 [0.1, 0.3, 0.6]\n   - Convergence: Truncate when \u2207L\u00b7h \u2261 \u03c4\u00b7\u2207P \u2265 \u03b3\n\n5. **Budget & timing:**\n   - Phase1 (feature detection): 10% budget\n   - Phase2 (transfer learning): 20% budget\n   - Phase3 (refinement): 50% budget\n   - Phase4 (fallback): 20% budget\n   - Time allocation: Feature detection completes when score improvement stalls\n\n6. **Validation & return contract**\n   - Feasibility checks:\n      - 0\u2264h[i]\u22641 for all i\n      - sum(h)=n/2 (tolerance 1e-6)\n      - Objective C(h) \u2261 np.max(corr)\n   - Re-evaluation: Use np.correlate(1.0-h, mode=\"full\")/dx\n   - Fallback: Return best solution from initial_h_values\n   - Additional checks:\n     - Plausibility: C(h) < 0.39\n     - Stability: \u2207L\u00b7\u2207R < 0.1\n     - Temporal consistency: move_av2(C(h)) < 0.5",
